✅ Difference Between Docker and Kubernetes:
| Feature        | **Docker**                                                 | **Kubernetes**                                                                               |
| -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Purpose**    | Docker is used to **create, package, and run containers**. | Kubernetes is used to **orchestrate (manage) multiple containers** across multiple machines. |
| **Level**      | Works at the **container level**.                          | Works at the **cluster level**.                                                              |
| **Use Case**   | Run one or a few containers on a single host.              | Run and manage **hundreds of containers** across many servers.                               |
| **Scaling**    | Manual or with Docker Swarm.                               | Automatic scaling, self-healing, and load balancing.                                         |
| **Networking** | Basic networking setup.                                    | Advanced service discovery and networking.                                                   |
| **Deployment** | You manage it manually or with basic scripts.              | Handles **rolling updates**, **restarts**, and **resource management** automatically.        |


💡 In short:
Docker is for creating and running containers.

Kubernetes is for managing and orchestrating many containers in a production-grade environment.

